#!/bin/bash
{{- if .claudecfg }}
# Installs herdr's own agent skill at ~/.claude/skills/herdr/SKILL.md, by asking
# the binary for it: `herdr --skill` prints the file on stdout.
#
# Generated rather than vendored, for the reason the wayfinder skills left this
# repo (see run_onchange_after_link-wf-skills.sh.tmpl): the prompt is part of the
# tool's interface, and an interface split across two repos on two release
# cadences drifts. Here it would drift in a way the agent cannot notice. The
# skill's own instruction is "the installed binary is the authority for command
# syntax", so a copy checked in at 0.8.2 would keep asserting flags and JSON
# paths that a later herdr renamed, while claiming the binary as its source.
#
# It cannot be a symlink the way wf's are: herdr has no `skills install`, and the
# skill exists nowhere on disk -- only as stdout from `--skill`. So it has to be
# written, which means re-written whenever the binary moves. That is what the
# fingerprint below is for, and it is the whole design problem here.
#
# A plain `run_after_` (every apply) was the obvious answer and is the wrong one.
# It works, but chezmoi treats an always-run script as a pending change forever:
# `chezmoi status` shows `R install-herdr-skill.sh` and `chezmoi diff` prints all
# ~95 lines of this file on every invocation. /sync opens with `chezmoi diff` to
# find files edited in ~ and re-add them, so a permanent 100-line diff is not
# cosmetic -- it buries the thing that read is for.
#
# So: run_onchange, keyed on the binary rather than on this script. run_onchange
# hashes the *rendered* script, and a `pixi global update herdr` changes nothing
# in a script that only names the command -- which is precisely the event this
# file exists to react to. Statting the binary into the rendered output puts that
# event into the hash.
#
# Why these paths, in this order:
#
#   ~/.pixi/envs/herdr/bin/herdr  the real binary from the toolbox manifest. It
#       has to be the env path, not ~/.pixi/bin/herdr: the latter is a pixi
#       trampoline, one hardlinked 766KB stub shared by all ~91 globals (check
#       `ls -l` and read the exe path out of
#       ~/.pixi/bin/trampoline_configuration/herdr.json). Its size and mtime are
#       identical for every tool and do not move when herdr does, so statting it
#       is a fingerprint of pixi, not of herdr.
#   ~/.local/bin/herdr            where herdr's own remote bootstrap installs on a
#       box first reached by `herdr --remote`, and where `herdr update` rewrites
#       it in place.
#   $(command -v herdr)           anything else -- a distro package, a manual
#       install -- as long as it is on the PATH of the shell running the apply.
#
# Coupling to pixi's env layout is a real cost and is accepted knowingly; the
# alternative is `output "herdr" "--skill" | sha256sum`, which is exact but aborts
# the *entire* apply if a future herdr ever exits non-zero for that flag. A stat
# cannot fail: `with` renders nothing when the path is absent, and the script
# below degrades to a printed notice.
#
# Not gated on .toolbox, unlike the server unit and the Claude hook. The real
# condition is "is there a herdr here", and the second and third paths above are
# exactly the machines where that is true without the manifest saying so. The
# binary probe below is therefore the gate, and it skips a container cleanly.
# .claudecfg is a separate question and does have to be asked -- writing a skill
# through somebody else's bind mount puts this machine's file in their ~/.claude.
#
# Harmless where herdr is installed but not running: the skill's first step is
# `test "${HERDR_ENV:-}" = 1`, and outside a herdr pane it stops there.
#
# herdr binary fingerprint (path, size, mtime) — the run_onchange hash key:
{{- $seen := list }}
{{- range concat (list (joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir ".pixi/envs/herdr/bin/herdr") (joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir ".local/bin/herdr")) (compact (list (lookPath "herdr"))) }}
{{-   $path := . }}
{{-   if not (has $path $seen) }}
{{-     $seen = append $seen $path }}
{{-     with stat $path }}
#   {{ $path }} {{ .size }} {{ .modTime }}
{{-     end }}
{{-   end }}
{{- end }}

set -euo pipefail

# chezmoi pins umask 0o022 for the files it manages (see .chezmoi.toml.tmpl), but a
# run_ script is not one of them: it writes with the umask of whichever shell ran
# the apply. Left alone this landed a 600 SKILL.md -- mktemp's mode, carried over
# by the copy -- and a 775 directory from a 0002 shell, next to 644/755 for every
# other skill. Pinned here so the mode is a property of this repo rather than of
# the terminal.
umask 022

# Where herdr actually is, in the same two places run_onchange_enable-herdr-server
# looks: ~/.pixi/bin from the toolbox manifest, ~/.local/bin from herdr's remote
# bootstrap. The trampoline is the right thing to *run*, unlike above where it was
# the wrong thing to stat. PATH last, since chezmoi inherits it from whichever
# shell ran the apply and install.sh's does not necessarily have pixi on it yet.
herdr_bin() {
    local candidate

    for candidate in "$HOME/.pixi/bin/herdr" "$HOME/.local/bin/herdr"; do
        if [ -x "$candidate" ]; then
            printf '%s\n' "$candidate"
            return 0
        fi
    done

    command -v herdr 2>/dev/null || true
}

herdr=$(herdr_bin)

if [ -z "$herdr" ]; then
    echo "herdr is not installed here — skipping its agent skill"
    exit 0
fi

skill_dir="$HOME/.claude/skills/herdr"
skill="$skill_dir/SKILL.md"

# --skill is a pure stdout dump, but it is still a binary this script does not
# own: a version that dropped the flag would print a usage error to stdout and
# exit 2, and writing that over a working skill is worse than keeping the old one.
staged=$(mktemp)
trap 'rm -f "$staged"' EXIT

if ! "$herdr" --skill >"$staged" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "\`$herdr --skill\` failed — leaving the existing herdr skill (if any) in place"
    exit 0
fi

if [ ! -s "$staged" ]; then
    echo "\`$herdr --skill\` printed nothing — leaving the existing herdr skill in place"
    exit 0
fi

if cmp -s "$staged" "$skill" 2>/dev/null; then
    exit 0
fi

mkdir -p "$skill_dir"
install -m 644 "$staged" "$skill"
echo "Installed the herdr agent skill from $herdr ($("$herdr" --version)) at $skill"
{{- else }}
# No-op: chezmoi does not own ~/.claude on this machine (claudecfg=false), so
# writing a skill into it would land this machine's file in another home through a
# bind mount. See the .tmpl in the chezmoi source for the full reasoning.
exit 0
{{- end }}
